course: dictionary
lesson: 6185
title: A word from old books
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong more to poems and old books than to everyday talk. Miry is one of them: it is a literary word, so you will meet it in stories, poems and even the Bible. Do not panic. Use the context around it, just as you would with any new word.
explain: «In winter cold and frost and miry ways kept them much indoors.» The clues are cold, frost and kept them indoors. People stayed inside because the roads were bad, so miry must mean very muddy. Check it by swapping in your meaning: "muddy ways kept them much indoors" still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: Miry comes from mire, which means deep, sticky mud. Something miry is full of mire. Writers can also use mirier and miriest to compare places, so a lane can be miry, a lane can be mirier, and the lane by the mill can be the miriest of all.
fact: Miry means very muddy and boggy, full of mire.
fact: Mire means deep, sticky mud.
fact: Miry is a literary word: writers use it in poems, old books and the Bible.
fact: Miry paths and roads are places where feet and wheels can get stuck.
fact: The comparative and superlative forms are mirier and miriest.
exercise: «In winter cold and frost and miry ways kept them much indoors.» What does miry mean? | Very old and dusty | Bright and sunny | *Very muddy and boggy | Sharp and pointed
exercise: Miry comes from a word meaning deep, sticky mud. What is mire? | Clear, cold water | *Deep, sticky mud | Tall, thick grass | Dry, dusty sand
exercise: Which path would a walker find hardest to cross? | A paved road with street lamps | A smooth, dry path in the park | A short path over a little bridge | *A miry path where boots sink into the mud
test: «The miriest lane in the village was the one by the old mill.» What does miriest mean? | Longest | Narrowest | *Most muddy and boggy | Brightest and cleanest
test: Why might a writer choose miry instead of muddy in an old poem? | It means the opposite of muddy | *It is a literary word that sounds old and paints a richer picture | It means very dry | It is only used for rivers
exam: Which word means deep, sticky mud, the word that miry comes from? | Marsh | *Mire | Miry | Mud
